<h2>The statutory scheme at a glance</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision (BNSS, 2023)</th><th>CrPC equivalent</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 367</td><td>Section 328</td><td>Magistrate holding an inquiry who has reason to believe the accused is of unsound mind and consequently incapable of making a defence must have the person examined by a civil surgeon or designated medical officer and postpone the proceeding.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 368</td><td>Section 329</td><td>The same duty at the stage of trial before a Magistrate or Court of Session: try the fact of unsoundness first, record a finding, and postpone the trial if the accused cannot make a defence.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 369</td><td>Section 330</td><td>Release of the person pending investigation or trial on security for care, safety and production — or, where that is not possible, detention in safe custody and reporting to the State Government.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Later provisions of the chapter</td><td>Sections 331–339</td><td>Resumption of the inquiry or trial on recovery of capacity, procedure where the accused appears to have been of sound mind, and the consequences of an acquittal on the ground of unsoundness.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Capacity first, custody second</h2>
<p>The sequence the chapter imposes is strict, and it is where most errors happen. The moment a court has reason to believe the accused cannot make a defence, the question of capacity must be decided before the prosecution evidence proceeds — on medical examination, not impression. A trial conducted against a person who could not understand it is no trial at all, and a conviction recorded that way is vulnerable in appeal. Conversely, the plea cannot be taken on counsel's assertion alone: the court tests it, and an accused found capable faces trial in the ordinary way, with the medical record available to answer any later repetition of the plea.</p>

<h2>Where bail fits in</h2>
<p>Three situations recur in practice, and each calls for a different instrument.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Fit to stand trial, mentally ill</h3><p>Mental illness short of incapacity does not trigger Chapter XXVII, but it remains highly relevant to ordinary bail under Sections 480 and 483 BNSS — as a circumstance bearing on custody hardship, treatment needs and the sick-and-infirm consideration the bail provisions recognise.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Incapable of defence, family able to care</h3><p>Here Section 369 is the natural route: release on security given by relatives who undertake to care for the person, prevent harm and produce them when required. The undertaking is the heart of the order — courts want a named person, an address and usually a treatment plan.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Incapable of defence, no one to care</h3><p>Where no adequate arrangement exists, the court orders safe custody. After the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 — which commands that persons with mental illness be treated, not warehoused — safe custody should mean a mental-health establishment, and counsel should press for a specific placement rather than a routine remand to jail.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Practical points for the defence</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Raise the capacity question at the first hearing where it is genuinely available — delay invites the suspicion of a tactical plea. Assemble the medical history before raising it: prior treatment records, disability certificates and prescriptions give the court something to send to the examining doctor. Propose the care arrangement in the application itself, naming the relative who will give security and the hospital that will treat. And remember the chapter postpones the case rather than ending it; a strategy built only on postponement leaves the accused under a pending charge indefinitely, so capacity findings should be revisited when treatment succeeds.</p>
</div>

<h2>Practical points for the complainant</h2>
<p>A complainant does not have to accept the plea at face value. The statute requires an examination, and the complainant may place material — the accused's conduct, employment, communications — suggesting capacity. Where release on security is ordered, the complainant can seek conditions protecting the victim, since the statutory security itself is directed at care and production, and can move for cancellation if the undertaking is breached.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this explainer states the statutory framework under the BNSS, 2023 for general information only. Individual cases turn on medical evidence and the offence alleged. It is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
